<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#doctrine-of-lapse">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Doctrine of lapse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1834">1834</a>–<a href="/years/1858">1858</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The doctrine of lapse was an annexation policy of the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, under which a princely state under its suzerainty was annexed to directly ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> if its ruler was manifestly incompetent or died without a male heir, overriding the long-established right of an heirless Indian sovereign to choose a successor. Articulated by the Company's directors as early as 1834, it is chiefly associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-dalhousie">Lord Dalhousie</a>, Governor-General from 1848 to 1856, who took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satara">Satara</a>, Jhansi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Tanjore</a> and other states by it. Widely considered illegitimate, it was one of the causes of the rebellion of 1857 and was renounced when Crown rule replaced the Company in 1858.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/doctrine-of-lapse.jpg" alt="Doctrine of lapse" width="330" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Richmond · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dalhousie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The policy was not Dalhousie's invention. In 1824 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> took over the princely state of Kittur when Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kittur-chennamma">Kittur Chennamma</a>, after the deaths of her husband and son, adopted a new son and tried to make him heir, which the British refused to accept. After the Court of Directors set out the doctrine early in 1834, the Company annexed Mandvi in 1839, Colaba and Jalaun in 1840, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a> in 1842, before Dalhousie became Governor-General.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Under Dalhousie the Company took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satara">Satara</a> in 1848, Jaitpur and Sambalpur in 1849, Baghal in 1850, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a> in 1852, Jhansi in 1853, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> in 1854, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Tanjore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcot">Arcot</a> in 1855. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>, annexed in 1856, is widely believed to have been taken under the doctrine, but Dalhousie annexed it on the pretext of misgovernment. By these annexations the Company added about four million pounds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pound-sterling">sterling</a> a year to its revenue. Udaipur had local princely rule restored in 1860.</p><p>By 1848 the British ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> and Bengal Presidencies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> directly and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> indirectly, and most remaining rulers were too weak to resist. Unwilling to spend large sums of money and soldiers, they had little choice but to give way.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Many Indians considered the doctrine illegitimate, and the resentment it caused was one of the causes of the uprising of 1857, when disbanded soldiers and others rallied behind the deposed dynasties. Elements of it survived independence. When Maharaja Rajendra Prakash of Sirmur died in late 1964 without a son or adopted heir, the Indian government held that the family's constitutional status had lapsed, though his senior widow adopted her daughter's son, and it did the same the following year for Akkalkot. Recognition of former ruling families ended in 1971 under the 26th Amendment, passed by the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indira-gandhi">Indira Gandhi</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Doctrine of lapse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
